<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="alignleft size-full wp-image-1085530" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-scaled.jpg" alt width="2154" height="2560" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-scaled.jpg 2154w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-303x360.jpg 303w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-862x1024.jpg 862w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-202x240.jpg 202w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-768x913.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-1292x1536.jpg 1292w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-1723x2048.jpg 1723w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/06081105/titus-300x357.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 2154px) 100vw, 2154px"><br>
Art may seem inessential to those who make the big decisions in times of crisis. But it never seemed more necessary to artists working in the time of plague. So it was 366 years ago when Rembrandt painted <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/object/Rembrandts-Son-Titus-in-a-Monks-Habit--dcccbbef997d02bb28627cb88e8feeed">a portrait of his son</a>, Titus, in a monk’s robe in 1660. Eight years later, Titus was dead from plague, which had only a few years earlier killed Hendrickje Stoffels, Rembrandt’s former housekeeper and second wife, who helped raise Titus, Rembrandt’s only child to survive into adulthood.</p>
<p>These unimaginable losses “contributed to the tragedy and anguish we see in Rembrandt’s late self-portraits,” <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2020/mar/17/plague-visionaries-how-rembrandt-titian-and-caravaggio-tackled-pestilence">writes <em>The Guardian</em>’s Jonathan Jones</a>. During the plague, Rembrandt also used his work as social critique.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>His etching <a href="https://collections.artsmia.org/art/55086/the-rat-poison-peddler-rembrandt-van-rijn"><em>The Rat-Poison Peddler</em></a>&nbsp;shows, “in a sense,” the Minneapolis Institute of Art’s Tom Rassieur tells the <em><a href="https://www.startribune.com/from-the-black-death-to-the-coronavirus-pandemics-can-also-yield-powerful-art/571801302/?refresh=true">Star Tribune</a>,</em> “the guy who purports to be helping—the exterminator—is probably doing as much to spread the disease as anyone else. That relates to [criticism] of our leadership today.” In his last years, Rembrandt painted self-portraits of his isolation and grief that found a new audience during the COVID lockdown.<br>
<img decoding="async" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-1085521" src="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer.jpg" alt width="1000" height="1127" srcset="https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer.jpg 1000w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer-319x360.jpg 319w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer-909x1024.jpg 909w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer-213x240.jpg 213w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer-768x866.jpg 768w, https://cdn8.openculture.com/2021/01/05232740/vermeer-300x338.jpg 300w" sizes="(max-width: 1000px) 100vw, 1000px"><br>
Elsewhere in the Netherlands, Rembrandt’s contemporary <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johannes_Vermeer">Johannes Vermeer</a> was no stranger to the kind of socially isolated world we found ourselves in during COVID. “His hometown of Delft was stricken with plague several times in the artist’s lifetime. In 1635 and 1636 over 2,000 people died, and in the mid-1650s and mid-1660s hundreds more,” <a href="https://www.cnn.com/style/article/vermeer-dutch-master-lockdown/index.html">notes Breeze Barrington at CNN</a>. The qualities we most associate with Vermeer’s work, the solitude and attentive presence, were developed during time spent in isolation.</p>
<p>“In this time of forced isolation,” Friso Lammertse, curator of 17th-century Dutch painting at the Rijksmuseum in Amsterdam, noted during the pandemic, Vermeer’s work “can point us at the fact that extreme beauty can be found just in our room.” The <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en">Rijksmuseum</a> didn’t just recommend art in the pandemic’s state of aloneness, but also doubled <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection">its collection of free, high-resolution works online</a>, by <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/node/Rembrandt-van-Rijn--ba2dc062acd137ffeac364321b7b10df">Rembrandt</a>, <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection/node/Johannes-Vermeer--5eb6c8bfe50817180452a6fcfb20510d">Vermeer</a>, and a host of other artists who used art to cope with loss and loneliness during the plagues of their times. The museum <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection">now offers 840,000 digitized images in total</a>.</p>
<p>The museum promised to “bring the museum to you,” and it has delivered not only with its <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection">extensive digital collection</a>, free for downloading, sharing and editing with a free Rijksmuseum account, but also with informative series on its website. Art is essential in the best and worst of times, when it shows us how to look closely at ourselves, our loved ones, and our surroundings, and treat life with more care and attention. Enter <a href="https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en/collection">the Rijksmuseum online collections here</a>.</p>
